<SiteLayout title="CourtVision — Basketball coaching platform | Bryan Kwan" description="A private basketball coaching platform connecting film review, source-synchronized 3D replay, shot maps, player profiles, and saved teaching moments." active="work">
  <section class="page-hero cv-page-hero"><div class="wrap"><a class="breadcrumb" href={href('work/')}>← All work</a><div class="product-hero-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow gold">01 / COMPUTER VISION & PRODUCT ENGINEERING</p><h1>CourtVision<span class="gold">.</span></h1><p class="product-tagline">The film. The floor.<br>The coaching conversation.</p><p class="lede">A private basketball platform bringing source-synchronized 3D replay, shot maps, player context, and saved teaching moments into one film room.</p><div class="tag-list"><span class="tag">Python / Computer vision</span><span class="tag">React / TypeScript</span><span class="tag">3D replay</span><span class="tag">Cloudflare</span></div></div><aside class="product-brief"><div><span>BUILT FOR</span><p>Coaches moving between game film, player context, and the next teaching moment.</p></div><div><span>MY CONTRIBUTION</span><p>The local vision pipeline, coaching interface, interactive replay, and private delivery workflow.</p></div><div><span>CURRENT STATUS</span><p>Hosted coaching platform with controlled access. Analysis and reconstruction capabilities continue to develop.</p></div></aside></div></div></section>
  <section class="wrap cv-walkthrough" id="walkthrough" aria-label="CourtVision interactive product walkthrough"><div class="walkthrough-heading"><p class="eyebrow gold">EXPLORE THE IDEA</p><p>Play a sequence. Inspect movement. Mark a moment.</p></div><CourtReplay client:visible compact /></section>
  <div class="wrap case-layout"><nav class="chapter-nav" aria-label="Case study chapters"><p>INSIDE COURTVISION</p><a href="#question">01 / The coaching problem</a><a href="#approach">02 / The workspace</a><a href="#evidence">03 / The system</a><a href="#limitations">04 / Current boundaries</a><a href="#artifacts">05 / Explore further</a></nav><article class="case-body">
    <section id="question"><h2>A useful answer starts<br>with the same moment.</h2><p>A coach should be able to find a possession, inspect the source film, understand the movement, and save a useful observation without losing context between tools.</p><p>CourtVision connects those steps. The interface stays anchored to the session and moment being reviewed, while the processing pipeline keeps source evidence and human corrections connected to the output.</p></section>
    <section id="approach"><h2>A workspace built around<br>the coaching workflow.</h2><div class="product-feature-grid"><div><span class="eyebrow gold">01 / FILM ROOM</span><h3>Find it. Watch it. Inspect it.</h3><p>A searchable shot queue, outcome filters, source-film playback, shot maps, and synchronized replay keep the evidence within reach.</p></div><div><span class="eyebrow gold">02 / SPATIAL REPLAY</span><h3>Follow the movement.</h3><p>Camera controls, player selection, movement history, and approximate spacing help inspect reconstructed sequences alongside their source film.</p></div><div><span class="eyebrow gold">03 / PLAYER CONTEXT</span><h3>Connect sessions to people.</h3><p>Searchable profiles, reviewed identity links, and practice, match, and season comparisons give a possession useful context.</p></div><div><span class="eyebrow gold">04 / COACH BOARD</span><h3>Keep the teaching moment.</h3><p>Court-anchored drawing, coaching notes, saved teaching moments, and annotated image exports turn an observation into something a coach can revisit.</p></div></div><div class="evidence-note"><strong>Carry the work forward.</strong><p>Session exports provide Excel shooting breakdowns and detailed CSV reports. Approved coaches can access the hosted viewer and synchronized notes across devices.</p></div></section>
    <section id="evidence"><h2>One product.<br>Two connected environments.</h2><div class="system-diagram" aria-label="Local processing and review feed a versioned private coaching viewer"><div><span class="eyebrow gold">LOCAL / PYTHON</span><h3>Process & review</h3><p>Person and ball detection<br>Tracking and calibration<br>Corrections and reconstruction</p></div><span class="system-connector" aria-hidden="true">→</span><div><span class="eyebrow gold">PRIVATE CLOUD / REACT</span><h3>Inspect & coach</h3><p>Film, replay, and player context<br>Notes and teaching moments<br>Excel and CSV exports</p></div></div><p>The processing workflow supports private resumable uploads, owner approval, local inference, checkpoint recovery, and review items. The hosted coaching viewer receives versioned packages after preparation and review.</p><p>Cloudflare Workers mediate access, D1 stores accounts and coaching documents, and private R2 storage holds session media. The separation lets coaches use the product without running the vision pipeline on their device.</p><div class="technical-details"><details><summary>What I had to get right</summary><ul><li>Keep source-film time and replay time aligned when moving between views.</li><li>Preserve workspace and session context across profiles, notes, and media.</li><li>Recover long processing runs without losing reviewed corrections.</li><li>Distinguish observed evidence from predicted or incomplete reconstruction.</li><li>Keep private media access separate from public source and interface delivery.</li></ul></details></div></section>
    <section id="limitations"><h2>Built to inspect.<br>Still being refined.</h2><p>The coaching product and the reconstruction models have different maturity levels. Film review, profiles, notes, exports, and controlled delivery form a working platform. Replay coverage and the quality of ball, pose, event, and identity reconstruction vary by source.</p><p>Full unattended reconstruction and reviewed publication remain in development. Rendered movement is not, by itself, proof of a correct ball path, named identity, shot outcome, or defensive assignment.</p><p>The interactive example on this page is authored with fictional players and movement. It demonstrates an interaction pattern; it is not a recording, accuracy benchmark, or replacement for the private CourtVision app.</p></section>
    <section id="artifacts"><h2>Let’s talk through the build.</h2><p>I can walk through the product decisions, processing architecture, and the work connecting an analytical pipeline to a coaching interface.</p><div class="resource-links"><a class="button button-dark" href={`mailto:${email}?subject=CourtVision%20project%20walkthrough`}>Discuss CourtVision <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="button" href="https://courtvision.bryanhkwan.workers.dev/v2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Coach sign in <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><p class="resource-caption">The hosted platform requires approved access. Team footage, named player records, private replay packages, and the private source repository are not published here.</p></section>
  </article></div><div class="wrap next-project"><a href={href("#arena-projects")} class="text-link">← Return to Home Court</a><a href={href('work/basketball/')} class="text-link">Next: the basketball decision platform <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
</SiteLayout>
